Email. that is, folks.

75
00:06:41,000 --> 00:06:53,000
And here's an answer to one of your emails.

76
00:06:53,000 --> 00:06:58,000
Paul Geisler got in touch with me and asked this question:

77
00:06:58,000 --> 00:07:09,000
What does the Patrol Leaders Council do when, on an outing, a majority of the patrol members of a particular patrol cannot attend due to a conflict in their schedules?

78
00:07:09,000 --> 00:07:12,000
Does the Patrol Leaders Council create hybrid patrols?

79
00:07:12,000 --> 00:07:18,000
I've heard of troops doing this, but it seems to me to be kind of going against the grain of the Patrol Method.

80
00:07:18,000 --> 00:07:21,000
You've talked in the past about larger patrols.

81
00:07:21,000 --> 00:07:25,000
This could help patrols have critical mass on a camping trip.

82
00:07:25,000 --> 00:07:33,000
Well, Paul, you know you've landed on one of those nagging questions about patrols that I haven't come up with a thoroughly foolproof answer for yet.

83
00:07:33,000 --> 00:07:38,000
My thinking is: patrol on a camping trip needs critical mass,

84
00:07:38,000 --> 00:07:41,000
And I kind of feel like that's five guys.

85
00:07:41,000 --> 00:07:49,000
It can happen with a little less, but the minimum seems to be, from my experience, around five guys,

86
00:07:49,000 --> 00:07:54,000
And of course that's all dependent on who those guys are and where they're at and everything.

87
00:07:54,000 --> 00:08:05,000
But just as a very general rule of thumb, that's where it seems to really be clicking along. when there's five to eight guys in a patrol on a camping trip,

88
00:08:05,000 --> 00:08:12,000
That's the ideal critical mass, and things are hardly ever ideal, are they?

89
00:08:12,000 --> 00:08:14,000
At least not in my experience.

90
00:08:14,000 --> 00:08:18,000
Making provisional patrols when needed is one answer

91
00:08:18,000 --> 00:08:24,000
I've never found completely satisfactory, but sometimes I'm stumped on what to do otherwise.

92
00:08:24,000 --> 00:08:39,000
I've seen patrols of three and even two. you know they function fine, but it just isn't really checking all the boxes of the kind of interactions and things you want to see in a patrol.

93
00:08:39,000 --> 00:08:52,000
Now, one way that seems to be promising that I'm going to talk about in another answer is the way that we've been doing our permission slips per quarter rather than trying to do them every month.

94
00:08:52,000 --> 00:08:57,000
I'm going to talk about that in a minute in the answer to another email question.

95
00:08:57,000 --> 00:09:07,000
But you know, one thing I've been trying to study these past few years is what adjustments we have to make to our application of the patrol method to adapt to the reality of our Scouts lives.

96
00:09:07,000 --> 00:09:15,000
The idea of the patrol is still absolutely relevant, but many of the assumptions on which it was originally based don't exist anymore.

97
00:09:15,000 --> 00:09:27,000
One assumption was that patrol would reflect kind of that natural neighborhood gang of the boys involved. but that neighborhood gang hasn't really been a factor for you know a long time,

98
00:09:27,000 --> 00:09:36,000
Because school districts are 10 times larger than they were in 1910, Scouts may be best friends with somebody who's 20 minutes driving distance away or more,

99
00:09:36,000 --> 00:09:43,000
And so those natural neighborhood connections, I think they're still very real in some places but in a lot of places they aren't.

100
00:09:43,000 --> 00:09:47,000
The patrol method was also built on the idea of friendly competition

101
00:09:48,000 --> 00:09:53,000
And I think now our Scouts value tolerance and cooperation over competition.

102
00:09:53,000 --> 00:10:04,000
I also think our Scouts have a much wider group of people they consider friends and fewer that they are willing to kind of sharply compete with.

103
00:10:04,000 --> 00:10:14,000
If I think about my life at Scout age- you know almost 40 years ago- and the circle of people that I considered my people, you know, my friends.

104
00:10:14,000 --> 00:10:23,000
That was a relatively small group. in comparison to what I seem to see in my Scouts today.

105
00:10:23,000 --> 00:10:33,000
They just seem to have a much broader group and they're not quite as tribal as I think that I was. when I was growing up-

106
00:10:33,000 --> 00:10:43,000
And I think I've mentioned this before- one of my youth leaders and I were talking about this once and his reflection was: it's a little difficult with these guys because they don't want to be broken up into little groups.

107
00:10:43,000 --> 00:10:45,000
They like being in a big group.

108
00:10:45,000 --> 00:10:48,000
Like I said, I'm still working on ideas about this.

109
00:10:48,000 --> 00:10:51,000
I have a promising. what I'm going to share with you in a moment,

110
00:10:51,000 --> 00:10:59,000
And I think what I encourage everybody to do as well as you, Paul, is to sit down with your youth leaders and discuss this,

111
00:10:59,000 --> 00:11:03,000
Listen carefully and see how they understand the way patrols work.

112
00:11:03,000 --> 00:11:10,000
Are patrols just logistical divisions for administrative purposes, or are they something more?

113
00:11:10,000 --> 00:11:14,000
What benefit do they see rising out of patrols and what problems do they see?

114
00:11:14,000 --> 00:11:15,000
What would they like to change?

115
00:11:15,000 --> 00:11:16,000
What would they like to strengthen?

116
00:11:16,000 --> 00:11:21,000
I would be really interested to hear what they have to say about that

117
00:11:21,000 --> 00:11:29,000
And then, you know, start a process of working towards addressing some of those concerns and meeting some of the goals that they express.

118
00:11:29,000 --> 00:11:35,000
Now listen, I don't think this is anything new or unprecedented in Scouting.

119
00:11:35,000 --> 00:11:43,000
I think things started changing as soon as the ink was dry on the signatures. that started the whole thing in 1910.

120
00:11:43,000 --> 00:11:50,000
And that it's been a challenge for Scouters and for organizations to keep up with what goes on ever since then.

121
00:11:50,000 --> 00:11:55,000
And we find good methods and we find good ways of making this work.

122
00:11:55,000 --> 00:12:02,000
But I think the understanding has to be: we can't base what we're doing today on the conditions that existed decades ago.

123
00:12:02,000 --> 00:12:07,000
And for us old guys, you know that's a big step to make.

124
00:12:07,000 --> 00:12:19,000
We have to actually inquire over what we're trying to achieve and how we're best going to achieve it, rather than whether or not we're going to preserve some kind of tradition or a habit of some kind.

125
00:12:19,000 --> 00:12:26,000
Because, yeah, I know, you know we're always more comfortable with doing things the way that they've always been done.

126
00:12:26,000 --> 00:12:31,000
But when we run into problems and we don't see results, that's when we should start asking questions, isn't it?

127
00:12:31,000 --> 00:12:36,000
This next email question? the sender asked to remain anonymous and said this:

128
00:12:36,000 --> 00:12:47,000
How do you keep your older Scouts interested in Scouting if you're at a point where your older Scouts are losing interest or have lost it entirely and just seem to want to punch their eagle card.

129
00:12:47,000 --> 00:12:52,000
Do you have a big serious meeting with them, or would that just turn them off even further?

130
00:12:52,000 --> 00:13:00,000
We're really trying to get our youth leadership motivated, but they're used to Scouts without that and they really don't know what to do.

131
00:13:00,000 --> 00:13:08,000
It's like kind of like we gave them the keys and we told them to drive, but they couldn't even make it out of the parking lot because they have no idea how a car works.

132
00:13:08,000 --> 00:13:12,000
I know just taking the wheel from them isn't the way, nor is backseat driving.

133
00:13:12,000 --> 00:13:17,000
So where do you start when you need to pretty much start at square one?

134
00:13:17,000 --> 00:13:20,000
Well, this is not an uncommon question.

135
00:13:21,000 --> 00:13:31,000
So you're a Scouter and the light kind of comes on and you say, oh, we should really have youth leadership taking care of a lot of stuff that we're taking care of now.

136
00:13:31,000 --> 00:13:35,000
We should really get the patrol method rolling and everything like that,

137
00:13:35,000 --> 00:13:40,000
And you're dedicated and you are definitely focused on making this happen and you're ready.

138
00:13:40,000 --> 00:13:46,000
And then you go and you try a couple of things and the Scouts look at it like a dog with a new pan and they go: what's this.

139
00:13:47,000 --> 00:13:49,000
You know we don't know how to do this.

140
00:13:49,000 --> 00:13:57,000
Well, imagine I'm coming over to your house and I'm going to say that the rules now say no more cooking in the kitchen.

141
00:13:57,000 --> 00:13:59,000
We're going to move the stove into the living room.

142
00:13:59,000 --> 00:14:02,000
We'll run a garden hose through the window, set a wash tub on the sofa-

143
00:14:02,000 --> 00:14:03,000
It'll be fine-

144
00:14:03,000 --> 00:14:06,000
And we're going to fill the kitchen sink with daisies.

145
00:14:06,000 --> 00:14:10,000
We're all going to move the fridge into the attic, because every good family has a fridge in the attic.

146
00:14:10,000 --> 00:14:13,000
You got to go up and down the stairs a few times, but you'll get used to it.

147
00:14:13,000 --> 00:14:16,000
I mean, is that going to? are you going to be happy about that?

148
00:14:16,000 --> 00:14:17,000
Of course not.

149
00:14:17,000 --> 00:14:24,000
You know it's. I'm just totally upset in every apple cart that you've got apples in, if you have an apple cart.

150
00:14:27,000 --> 00:14:32,000
So what I'm saying is is that that is the way Scouts are going to look on this.

151
00:14:32,000 --> 00:14:38,000
If they've been around for four or five years or three or four years and they're used to the things happening one way

152
00:14:38,000 --> 00:14:41,000
and you decide you're going to change it all of a sudden,

153
00:14:41,000 --> 00:14:48,000
they're going to react the same way that I would. that you would react if I came up to your house and put your kitchen, in the living room.

154
00:14:48,000 --> 00:14:50,000
You understand what needs to be done,

155
00:14:50,000 --> 00:14:53,000
You understand what you want to see. but they have no idea.

156
00:14:53,000 --> 00:14:56,000
And if you just roll in with a whole bunch of changes all at once,

157
00:14:56,000 --> 00:14:59,000
you know that's not going to work real well

158
00:14:59,000 --> 00:15:04,000
And it's going to frustrate your Scouts. and it's going to frustrate you because you think,

159
00:15:04,000 --> 00:15:08,000
oh, they'll get this right away, because it's so much better,

160
00:15:08,000 --> 00:15:09,000
It's going to be great,

161
00:15:09,000 --> 00:15:15,000
They're pretty fast on the uptake. but you can't just roll in with a whole bunch of changes all at once

162
00:15:15,000 --> 00:15:21,000
and expect them to understand or to get excited about all those changes.

163
00:15:21,000 --> 00:15:24,000
I mean, you mentioned that they just want to. in your perception,

164
00:15:24,000 --> 00:15:27,000
they just want to hang out and punch their evil card.

165
00:15:27,000 --> 00:15:32,000
Well, that's because that's what the last group of older Scouts probably did, and likely the group before that.

166
00:15:32,000 --> 00:15:33,000
That's all they have to go on.

167
00:15:33,000 --> 00:15:36,000
And it's important that you don't blame them and you don't cop an attitude.

168
00:15:36,000 --> 00:15:39,000
They're only doing what they know.

169
00:15:39,000 --> 00:15:45,000
So moving a troop into a youth led model can't be just tossing all the older Scouts into the deep end of the pool.

170
00:15:45,000 --> 00:15:50,000
You've got to take them by the hand and kind of wade in slowly from the shallow end

171
00:15:50,000 --> 00:15:56,000
Small steps supported with lots of encouragement, success in small things that you build on

172
00:15:56,000 --> 00:16:00,000
happy Scouts, and that's an indication that you're traveling at the right speed.

173
00:16:00,000 --> 00:16:05,000
When stuff doesn't take hold and when things kind of fall apart a little bit,

174
00:16:05,000 --> 00:16:08,000
well, that's an indication maybe they aren't getting the message

175
00:16:08,000 --> 00:16:11,000
and you aren't doing a very good job of getting the message across

176
00:16:11,000 --> 00:16:14,000
and you're probably trying to move too fast.

177
00:16:14,000 --> 00:16:18,000
So my best advice is: set these guys up to succeed.

178
00:16:18,000 --> 00:16:23,000
Help them, take them by the hand, walk them through the whole process a few times

179
00:16:23,000 --> 00:16:28,000
and understand. some of them may not want to do it and may not get it,

180
00:16:28,000 --> 00:16:30,000
and they don't want to see things changed.

181
00:16:30,000 --> 00:16:35,000
The other thing to do is to work with the other volunteers involved, with the troop,

182
00:16:35,000 --> 00:16:38,000
and decide what's an adult responsibility and a scout responsibility.

183
00:16:38,000 --> 00:16:42,000
Planning is a really tiny slice of what happens. Planning is easy.

184
00:16:42,000 --> 00:16:46,000
Don't put too high a premium on planning or the origination of ideas.

185
00:16:46,000 --> 00:16:51,000
If you throw a calendar down on the table and you tell the Scouts to plan something,

186
00:16:51,000 --> 00:16:52,000
they're going to be befuddled.

187
00:16:52,000 --> 00:16:56,000
Emphasize preparation and execution of the plan.

188
00:16:56,000 --> 00:16:58,000
Get them more involved in that.

189
00:16:58,000 --> 00:17:02,000
Look at what adults are doing now and what you'd like to see Scouts doing

190
00:17:02,000 --> 00:17:06,000
and go in steps to make that happen.

191
00:17:06,000 --> 00:17:10,000
And the final bit of advice about this is: you cannot compare

192
00:17:10,000 --> 00:17:13,000
the way that they're going to perform as youth leaders

193
00:17:13,000 --> 00:17:17,000
to the way that adults perform when they were doing all of that work.

194
00:17:17,000 --> 00:17:21,000
Reset your expectations of success and failure.

195
00:17:21,000 --> 00:17:24,000
Lower the bar a bit- because these are young people

196
00:17:24,000 --> 00:17:28,000
very often making their first forays into this idea

197
00:17:28,000 --> 00:17:33,000
of a real responsibility and leadership for something like this-

198
00:17:33,000 --> 00:17:39,000
And make sure that your level of expectation is keyed in

199
00:17:39,000 --> 00:17:44,000
to the idea of them succeeding in very small things at first,

200
00:17:44,000 --> 00:17:45,000
and then you build on it,

201
00:17:45,000 --> 00:17:48,000
Congratulate them for doing a great job,

202
00:17:48,000 --> 00:17:51,000
Bump up the expectation a little bit and repeat.

203
00:17:51,000 --> 00:17:54,000
When you're in a situation like this, where the adults

204
00:17:54,000 --> 00:17:56,000
have been doing everything and you really want to see

205
00:17:56,000 --> 00:17:59,000
the youth leaders take over, it isn't simply passing the baton

206
00:17:59,000 --> 00:18:00,000
or throwing a switch.

207
00:18:00,000 --> 00:18:04,000
There's a transitional period for the Scouts and the adults

208
00:18:04,000 --> 00:18:07,000
to begin to learn a new set of expectations and skills,

209
00:18:07,000 --> 00:18:09,000
And if they're failing to do so, it's not indicative

210
00:18:09,000 --> 00:18:14,000
of a lack of interest or talent or initiative on their part.

211
00:18:14,000 --> 00:18:17,000
It's more likely you're not giving them enough time

212
00:18:17,000 --> 00:18:19,000
to understand what's going on.

213
00:18:19,000 --> 00:18:22,000
Maybe you're not giving them enough support

214
00:18:22,000 --> 00:18:25,000
and you might be moving a little bit too fast.

215
00:18:25,000 --> 00:18:27,000
I had this email from Timo Sopeta,

216
00:18:27,000 --> 00:18:31,000
who's the Scoutmaster of Troop 343 in Selma, Texas.

217
00:18:31,000 --> 00:18:36,000
You mentioned in a recent podcast a quarterly program model.

218
00:18:36,000 --> 00:18:39,000
I'm extremely interested in what this means.

219
00:18:39,000 --> 00:18:42,000
It sounds like something that we need in our troop.

220
00:18:42,000 --> 00:18:45,000
Well, I have been kind of nibbling around the edges of this

221
00:18:45,000 --> 00:18:48,000
and I'm prepared to talk about it a little bit more.

222
00:18:48,000 --> 00:18:53,000
I've been hesitant to because we just started doing this last fall,

223
00:18:53,000 --> 00:18:56,000
So we've only been through a couple of evolutions with it.

224
00:18:56,000 --> 00:18:59,000
But let me explain what I'm talking about.

225
00:18:59,000 --> 00:19:02,000
The quarterly plan idea is really very simple.

226
00:19:02,000 --> 00:19:07,000
So what we did is we took the year and divided it into quarters,

227
00:19:07,000 --> 00:19:11,000
very closely aligned with the quarters that the school district has.

228
00:19:11,000 --> 00:19:14,000
So our first quarter, I think, begins in September.

229
00:19:14,000 --> 00:19:17,000
So we go September, October, November, then December, January,

230
00:19:17,000 --> 00:19:21,000
February, then March, April, May, then June, July, August-

231
00:19:21,000 --> 00:19:23,000
Those four quarters.

232
00:19:23,000 --> 00:19:26,000
So 30 days in advance of each quarter

233
00:19:26,000 --> 00:19:29,000
we have a parents meeting in a court of honor,

234
00:19:29,000 --> 00:19:32,000
which is a good thing to do every quarter anyway.

235
00:19:32,000 --> 00:19:36,000
What we do at that meeting is we hand out the permission slip

236
00:19:36,000 --> 00:19:38,000
for the next quarter,

237
00:19:38,000 --> 00:19:40,000
And the permission slip has all the details

238
00:19:40,000 --> 00:19:43,000
for the next three camping trips.

239
00:19:43,000 --> 00:19:46,000
And when I say all the details, I'm telling a bit of,

240
00:19:46,000 --> 00:19:48,000
I'm telling a little bit of a fib.

241
00:19:48,000 --> 00:19:49,000
Okay,

242
00:19:49,000 --> 00:19:52,000
It has the dates for the next three camping trips,

243
00:19:52,000 --> 00:19:57,000
the target, that activity that we have planned, and the place,

244
00:19:57,000 --> 00:19:59,000
Because, to be completely honest with you,

245
00:19:59,000 --> 00:20:03,000
we haven't had every single detail for those trips ironed out

246
00:20:03,000 --> 00:20:06,000
three months ahead of time. but we do have dates.

247
00:20:06,000 --> 00:20:10,000
So we hand that permission slip out with all those details on it

248
00:20:10,000 --> 00:20:13,000
And at that meeting 30 days ahead of the beginning

249
00:20:13,000 --> 00:20:15,000
of the next quarter,

250
00:20:15,000 --> 00:20:18,000
And then we ask for those permission slips to be returned

251
00:20:18,000 --> 00:20:21,000
to us within two weeks time.

252
00:20:21,000 --> 00:20:25,000
The idea is that 100% of the families will return those

253
00:20:25,000 --> 00:20:29,000
permission slips to us telling us what camping trips

254
00:20:29,000 --> 00:20:32,000
they are making a commitment for and which ones

255
00:20:32,000 --> 00:20:34,000
they will not be attending.

256
00:20:34,000 --> 00:20:37,000
The idea is 100% within two weeks.

257
00:20:37,000 --> 00:20:39,000
We haven't actually hit that.

258
00:20:39,000 --> 00:20:43,000
We've gotten like 80 or 85%, which is real good,

259
00:20:43,000 --> 00:20:46,000
but we're only about three evolutions into this

260
00:20:46,000 --> 00:20:50,000
And you know we're learning along the way that we have

261
00:20:50,000 --> 00:20:54,000
to do an awful lot of work to get those permission slips

262
00:20:54,000 --> 00:20:56,000
back within two weeks time.

263
00:20:56,000 --> 00:20:58,000
So, once we have the commitments,

264
00:20:58,000 --> 00:21:01,000
we're still two weeks in front of the beginning

265
00:21:01,000 --> 00:21:02,000
of the quarter

266
00:21:02,000 --> 00:21:05,000
And that means we're anywhere from four to six weeks

267
00:21:05,000 --> 00:21:08,000
out for the first camping trip in that quarter.

268
00:21:08,000 --> 00:21:11,000
Once we have those commitments, we take that to the patrol

269
00:21:11,000 --> 00:21:14,000
leaders council and we discuss whether or not any

270
00:21:14,000 --> 00:21:17,000
changes need to be made to the way that patrols are

271
00:21:17,000 --> 00:21:19,000
set up. at present,

272
00:21:19,000 --> 00:21:22,000
For instance, there may be one patrol with only two guys

273
00:21:22,000 --> 00:21:25,000
on one of the camping trips and three guys

274
00:21:25,000 --> 00:21:27,000
going on the other two

275
00:21:27,000 --> 00:21:29,000
And we say maybe we should shuffle patrols around

276
00:21:29,000 --> 00:21:32,000
a little bit for this quarter so that we have more guys

277
00:21:32,000 --> 00:21:35,000
in patrols going on camping trips and making

278
00:21:35,000 --> 00:21:37,000
preparations and things like that.

279
00:21:37,000 --> 00:21:40,000
So far I don't think we've made any major changes

280
00:21:40,000 --> 00:21:42,000
to the way patrols are.

281
00:21:42,000 --> 00:21:45,000
But you understand what the point is, and this references

282
00:21:45,000 --> 00:21:48,000
an earlier email answer in this podcast.

283
00:21:48,000 --> 00:21:50,000
What do you do when you have two or three guys

284
00:21:50,000 --> 00:21:52,000
from a patrol going on a camping trip?

285
00:21:52,000 --> 00:21:56,000
The only real way that I've been able to fix that

286
00:21:56,000 --> 00:21:59,000
so far is to totally avoid it by asking everybody

287
00:21:59,000 --> 00:22:02,000
way up front what their commitments are

288
00:22:02,000 --> 00:22:04,000
for those three months.

289
00:22:04,000 --> 00:22:06,000
And, like I said, it hasn't caused us to make a whole

290
00:22:06,000 --> 00:22:08,000
lot of adjustments

291
00:22:08,000 --> 00:22:11,000
This most recent quarter. we really kind of fell down

292
00:22:11,000 --> 00:22:13,000
on the job of getting all the commitments back

293
00:22:13,000 --> 00:22:16,000
on time, so we weren't able to go through this

294
00:22:16,000 --> 00:22:17,000
process.

295
00:22:17,000 --> 00:22:19,000
It's a little bit of a mess,

296
00:22:19,000 --> 00:22:21,000
But hey, a little bit of a mess is better than a

297
00:22:21,000 --> 00:22:23,000
total mess.

298
00:22:23,000 --> 00:22:26,000
So you know, we're soldiering on with it.

299
00:22:26,000 --> 00:22:30,000
The other thing we do is we look at that roster

300
00:22:30,000 --> 00:22:32,000
of commitments and we ask the patrol leaders

301
00:22:32,000 --> 00:22:35,000
council if we need to have patrol leader

302
00:22:35,000 --> 00:22:38,000
elections and we talk about who's going to

303
00:22:38,000 --> 00:22:40,000
occupy those leadership roles for the coming

304
00:22:40,000 --> 00:22:41,000
quarter.

305
00:22:41,000 --> 00:22:43,000
For many years our senior patrol leader was

306
00:22:43,000 --> 00:22:45,000
elected on an annual basis,

307
00:22:45,000 --> 00:22:46,000
Seemed to work,

308
00:22:46,000 --> 00:22:48,000
And then we decided that we would cut that down

309
00:22:48,000 --> 00:22:50,000
to six months at a time, and that worked fine.

310
00:22:50,000 --> 00:22:52,000
And part of the implementation of this

311
00:22:52,000 --> 00:22:55,000
quarterly program was that we would elect a new

312
00:22:55,000 --> 00:22:57,000
senior patrol leader every three months.

313
00:22:57,000 --> 00:22:59,000
Now that's going to make some people's head

314
00:22:59,000 --> 00:23:00,000
explode,

315
00:23:00,000 --> 00:23:02,000
But one of the things that we're trying to

316
00:23:02,000 --> 00:23:04,000
address is the way that electing a senior

317
00:23:04,000 --> 00:23:06,000
patrol leader who had the best of intentions

318
00:23:06,000 --> 00:23:09,000
and really wanted to do the job and was a

319
00:23:09,000 --> 00:23:13,000
great pick, but whose schedule prevented them

320
00:23:13,000 --> 00:23:16,000
from going on 50% of the camping trips for

321
00:23:16,000 --> 00:23:18,000
the time that they were senior patrol leader,

322
00:23:18,000 --> 00:23:21,000
It just didn't. it didn't seem satisfactory for

323
00:23:21,000 --> 00:23:22,000
everybody involved.

324
00:23:22,000 --> 00:23:24,000
So now we have this rule of thumb,

325
00:23:24,000 --> 00:23:26,000
It would be a good idea to elect a senior

326
00:23:26,000 --> 00:23:28,000
patrol leader who's going to be on the next

327
00:23:28,000 --> 00:23:30,000
three camping trips and who's going to be

328
00:23:30,000 --> 00:23:32,000
available to make all the troop meetings for

329
00:23:32,000 --> 00:23:33,000
the next three months.

330
00:23:33,000 --> 00:23:35,000
And this perhaps has been one of the better

331
00:23:35,000 --> 00:23:38,000
outcomes of this system, because last fall,

332
00:23:38,000 --> 00:23:41,000
in the way that things go, I had four or

333
00:23:41,000 --> 00:23:43,000
five older scouts who would normally be

334
00:23:43,000 --> 00:23:45,000
considered as potential senior patrol

335
00:23:45,000 --> 00:23:47,000
leaders, were participating in the marching

336
00:23:47,000 --> 00:23:48,000
band,

337
00:23:48,000 --> 00:23:50,000
And if you know anything about marching

338
00:23:50,000 --> 00:23:52,000
band, at least around here, was like a

339
00:23:52,000 --> 00:23:54,000
very time intensive commitment where they

340
00:23:54,000 --> 00:23:56,000
couldn't make troop meetings and they

341
00:23:56,000 --> 00:23:58,000
weren't going to be on any outings because

342
00:23:58,000 --> 00:24:00,000
on Saturdays they were off in a parade

343
00:24:00,000 --> 00:24:02,000
or at a football game.

344
00:24:02,000 --> 00:24:04,000
On Monday nights- our troop meeting

345
00:24:04,000 --> 00:24:06,000
nights- they had rehearsals, and if we

346
00:24:06,000 --> 00:24:08,000
had done what we normally do and just

347
00:24:08,000 --> 00:24:10,000
elected a senior patrol leader for the

348
00:24:10,000 --> 00:24:11,000
next six months, it probably would have

349
00:24:11,000 --> 00:24:12,000
been one of the guys in the marching

350
00:24:12,000 --> 00:24:14,000
band and then he would have disappeared.

351
00:24:14,000 --> 00:24:16,000
And whose fault would that be?

352
00:24:16,000 --> 00:24:18,000
You know? I mean, is it the senior

353
00:24:18,000 --> 00:24:19,000
patrol leader's fault?

354
00:24:19,000 --> 00:24:21,000
Not really, because I don't expect them

355
00:24:21,000 --> 00:24:23,000
to be thinking that way, because I know

356
00:24:23,000 --> 00:24:24,000
that they don't.

357
00:24:24,000 --> 00:24:26,000
So the idea of asking them,

358
00:24:26,000 --> 00:24:27,000
hey, are you going to be on the next

359
00:24:27,000 --> 00:24:28,000
three camping trips,

360
00:24:28,000 --> 00:24:29,000
Are you going to be able to make the

361
00:24:29,000 --> 00:24:30,000
meetings?

362
00:24:30,000 --> 00:24:31,000
And getting their parents involved in

363
00:24:31,000 --> 00:24:32,000
that- because parents are a little

364
00:24:32,000 --> 00:24:34,000
smarter about schedules sometimes,

365
00:24:34,000 --> 00:24:36,000
Notice, I said sometimes-

366
00:24:36,000 --> 00:24:38,000
It really has worked very, very well

367
00:24:38,000 --> 00:24:39,000
for us.

368
00:24:39,000 --> 00:24:41,000
The other thing is that we now have

369
00:24:41,000 --> 00:24:43,000
twice the number of opportunities for

370
00:24:43,000 --> 00:24:44,000
somebody to be the senior patrol

371
00:24:44,000 --> 00:24:46,000
leader and we have a pretty deep bench

372
00:24:46,000 --> 00:24:48,000
in the way that that goes. right now.

373
00:24:48,000 --> 00:24:50,000
We have a lot of guys who have a

374
00:24:50,000 --> 00:24:52,000
lot of good leadership potential who

375
00:24:52,000 --> 00:24:53,000
can fulfill that role.

376
00:24:53,000 --> 00:24:55,000
Since we implemented this,

377
00:24:55,000 --> 00:24:57,000
a couple of things that have happened

378
00:24:57,000 --> 00:24:59,000
that were very unlikely to

379
00:24:59,000 --> 00:25:01,000
happen in the past:

380
00:25:01,000 --> 00:25:03,000
100% attendance from the senior

381
00:25:03,000 --> 00:25:05,000
patrol leader on every camping trip in

382
00:25:05,000 --> 00:25:07,000
the past six or eight months. nearly

383
00:25:07,000 --> 00:25:09,000
100% from patrol leaders. almost

384
00:25:09,000 --> 00:25:11,000
100% attendance of the senior

385
00:25:11,000 --> 00:25:14,000
patrol leader at troop meetings

386
00:25:14,000 --> 00:25:16,000
and patrols that are at critical

387
00:25:16,000 --> 00:25:18,000
mass for camping trips.

388
00:25:18,000 --> 00:25:20,000
The other thing that has proven to

389
00:25:20,000 --> 00:25:22,000
be very valuable with this is, if

390
00:25:22,000 --> 00:25:24,000
we have commitments that far out,

391
00:25:24,000 --> 00:25:26,000
we can adjust the logistics for a

392
00:25:26,000 --> 00:25:27,000
trip.

393
00:25:27,000 --> 00:25:28,000
No waiting until the week of the

394
00:25:28,000 --> 00:25:29,000
trip to find out that there are

395
00:25:29,000 --> 00:25:31,000
too few scouts attending.

396
00:25:31,000 --> 00:25:32,000
And, by the way, the senior patrol

397
00:25:32,000 --> 00:25:33,000
leader and patrol leaders have a

398
00:25:33,000 --> 00:25:34,000
cross commitment.

399
00:25:34,000 --> 00:25:35,000
They can't go.

400
00:25:35,000 --> 00:25:36,000
There was backpacking trip where

401
00:25:36,000 --> 00:25:38,000
we needed to arrange a bus

402
00:25:38,000 --> 00:25:40,000
charter and we have to have

403
00:25:40,000 --> 00:25:42,000
critical mass to be able to do

404
00:25:42,000 --> 00:25:43,000
that.

405
00:25:43,000 --> 00:25:44,000
Well, we knew that we had it

406
00:25:44,000 --> 00:25:45,000
quite a ways out,

407
00:25:45,000 --> 00:25:47,000
So that was supportable.

408
00:25:47,000 --> 00:25:49,000
And finally, the other

409
00:25:49,000 --> 00:25:51,000
plus is that the families

410
00:25:51,000 --> 00:25:53,000
are growing to appreciate the idea

411
00:25:53,000 --> 00:25:54,000
that we're doing this.

412
00:25:54,000 --> 00:25:56,000
It cuts down on our administrative

413
00:25:56,000 --> 00:25:58,000
burden because instead of

414
00:25:58,000 --> 00:26:00,000
trying to collect a whole

415
00:26:00,000 --> 00:26:02,000
bunch of paper and payments every

416
00:26:02,000 --> 00:26:04,000
month, now we're doing it once every

417
00:26:04,000 --> 00:26:05,000
three months.

418
00:26:05,000 --> 00:26:06,000
So if parents are writing a check,

419
00:26:06,000 --> 00:26:08,000
they write a check once every three

420
00:26:08,000 --> 00:26:09,000
months instead of every month,

421
00:26:09,000 --> 00:26:11,000
And it's made life a

422
00:26:11,000 --> 00:26:12,000
lot simpler that way.

423
00:26:12,000 --> 00:26:14,000
So that's kind of

424
00:26:14,000 --> 00:26:16,000
an overview of the quarterly

425
00:26:16,000 --> 00:26:17,000
plan.

426
00:26:17,000 --> 00:26:18,000
We made a commitment to follow it

427
00:26:18,000 --> 00:26:19,000
through for a year

428
00:26:19,000 --> 00:26:21,000
and to assess it at the end of

429
00:26:21,000 --> 00:26:22,000
the year and see if it was

430
00:26:22,000 --> 00:26:23,000
something we wanted to continue

431
00:26:23,000 --> 00:26:24,000
with,

432
00:26:24,000 --> 00:26:26,000
And thus far I believe that it's

433
00:26:26,000 --> 00:26:28,000
working really well and I hope

434
00:26:28,000 --> 00:26:30,000
to see it continue.

435
00:26:30,000 --> 00:26:32,000
One last email from Kevin

436
00:26:32,000 --> 00:26:34,000
Ames who asked this

437
00:26:34,000 --> 00:26:35,000
question.

438
00:26:35,000 --> 00:26:36,000
I'm the scout master of a new

439
00:26:36,000 --> 00:26:38,000
scout trip, but I've been a

440
00:26:38,000 --> 00:26:39,000
scouter for a while now.

441
00:26:39,000 --> 00:26:40,000
I've found a lot of

442
00:26:40,000 --> 00:26:42,000
information on the positions of

443
00:26:42,000 --> 00:26:43,000
responsibility. with the exception

444
00:26:43,000 --> 00:26:45,000
of junior assistant scout master,

445
00:26:45,000 --> 00:26:46,000
I haven't been able to find a

446
00:26:46,000 --> 00:26:47,000
whole lot of information, ideas

447
00:26:47,000 --> 00:26:49,000
how this position can best be

448
00:26:49,000 --> 00:26:50,000
used.

449
00:26:50,000 --> 00:26:51,000
I presently have an Eagle

450
00:26:51,000 --> 00:26:52,000
Scout who wants to remain

451
00:26:52,000 --> 00:26:53,000
active with the troop in this

452
00:26:53,000 --> 00:26:55,000
position, but I'm unsure how

453
00:26:55,000 --> 00:26:56,000
it's going to work.

454
00:26:56,000 --> 00:26:58,000
Give me some ideas on how I

455
00:26:58,000 --> 00:26:59,000
should treat this role of

456
00:26:59,000 --> 00:27:00,000
responsibility.

457
00:27:00,000 --> 00:27:01,000
Well, Kevin, that's an

458
00:27:01,000 --> 00:27:02,000
excellent question

459
00:27:02,000 --> 00:27:04,000
And it's one that actually

460
00:27:04,000 --> 00:27:05,000
I'm not going to try and

461
00:27:05,000 --> 00:27:06,000
answer on the podcast.

462
00:27:06,000 --> 00:27:07,000
I'm going to answer in a

463
00:27:07,000 --> 00:27:09,000
blog post that should be

464
00:27:09,000 --> 00:27:11,000
up within a day or two of

465
00:27:11,000 --> 00:27:13,000
the podcast publishing.

466
00:27:13,000 --> 00:27:15,000
So keep your eyes open and

467
00:27:15,000 --> 00:27:17,000
watch scoutmastercgcom for a

468
00:27:17,000 --> 00:27:19,000
post titled What is a

469
00:27:19,000 --> 00:27:21,000
junior assistant scout master?

470
00:27:21,000 --> 00:27:22,000
It's been something I've been

471
00:27:22,000 --> 00:27:23,000
working on in my troop during

472
00:27:23,000 --> 00:27:24,000
the past several months.

473
00:27:24,000 --> 00:27:25,000
I have some exciting stuff to

474
00:27:25,000 --> 00:27:26,000
share about that,

475
00:27:26,000 --> 00:27:28,000
So check that post out a

